  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Dark Mode Issues**\n\nOkay, I\'m now digging into the dark mode implementation, specifically focusing on the toggle functionality and any potential rendering problems with light mode. My current line of inquiry is inspecting how Tailwind CSS v4 handles the `dark:` variant. I am looking into how `@import \"tailwindcss\";` in `src/index.css` is handling the default dark variant, to start.\n\n\n**Confirming Dark Mode Configuration**\n\nI\'ve confirmed a crucial point: Tailwind CSS v4\'s default behavior, without a custom variant configuration, ties `dark:` classes directly to the `prefers-color-scheme: dark` media query. This means, the simple addition or removal of the `.dark` class on the `<html>` element becomes entirely ineffective. The browser will always use the system\'s dark mode setting! This setup clearly defeats any class-based dark mode toggle. I am now double-checking whether `@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));` or `@variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));` is the right directive for a class-based implementation.\n\n\n**Testing Dark Mode Toggle**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the application\'s state and how it interacts with the DOM. Specifically, I\'m verifying how the `darkMode` state in `App.tsx` directly affects the `<html>` element\'s class. I see `useEffect` updates the class correctly, adding or removing \'dark\'. I\'m also confirming the synchronization between the `darkMode` state and the presence of the \'dark\' class. The next step is to examine whether hardcoded colors bypass the class-based toggle and whether the dark mode media query is causing overrides. I want to test adding `@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));` to `src/index.css` and running `compile_applet`. I also need to view `src/index.css`.\n\n\n**Inspecting CSS and Components**\n\nI\'ve added the `@custom-variant` directive and checked `src/index.css`. Now, I\'m auditing components within `src/components/` and `src/App.tsx` for hardcoded color values. My focus is on identifying any instances of direct color assignment without a `dark:` prefix or light mode colors that conflict. I\'m investigating each file individually to see if there are missing `dark:` variants.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 983927675
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
