    created_at {
      seconds: 1786444406
      nanos: 310154652
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "You are a senior product designer, UI/UX designer, and frontend design engineer.\n\nI am building a modern SaaS application for content creators. The application helps creators connect their Google Drive, discover and organize their content, generate high-quality social media captions with AI, edit content, and prepare posts for publishing.\n\nUse the **Kickpush Design Studio website on Awwwards** as the primary visual inspiration:\n\nhttps://www.awwwards.com/sites/kickpush-design-studio\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo NOT copy the Kickpush website.\n\nDo NOT reproduce its exact layouts, text, branding, illustrations, images, or proprietary assets.\n\nInstead, extract and translate its **design principles, visual confidence, typography, spacing, composition, color usage, minimalism, and creative-agency aesthetic** into a completely original SaaS application.\n\nThe final product should feel like a premium digital product designed by an award-winning creative studio \342\200\224 NOT like a generic AI-generated dashboard.\n\n---\n\n# 1. CORE DESIGN DIRECTION\n\nThe visual identity should be:\n\n* Minimal\n* Bold\n* Editorial\n* Creative\n* Typography-driven\n* Confident\n* Modern\n* Premium\n* Slightly unconventional\n* Highly intentional\n* Clean without feeling boring\n* Playful without becoming childish\n* Professional without feeling corporate\n\nThe interface should communicate:\n\n> \"This is a beautifully designed creative tool.\"\n\nIt should NOT communicate:\n\n> \"This is another AI SaaS template.\"\n\n---\n\n# 2. ABSOLUTELY AVOID GENERIC AI UI\n\nDo NOT use:\n\n* Purple-to-blue gradients\n* Generic AI dashboard aesthetics\n* Excessive glassmorphism\n* Floating gradient blobs\n* Excessive shadows\n* Excessive rounded cards\n* Every section inside a card\n* Huge collections of identical cards\n* Generic SaaS templates\n* Excessive icons\n* Decorative UI that has no purpose\n* Random gradients\n* Neon effects\n* Excessive pills\n* Excessive badges\n* Overly rounded buttons\n* Stock-looking illustrations\n* AI-generated decorative graphics\n\nDo not make every component look like a floating white card.\n\nUse **space, typography, borders, lines, scale, color blocks, imagery and composition** to create hierarchy.\n\n---\n\n# 3. VISUAL PHILOSOPHY\n\nTypography should do a significant amount of the visual work.\n\nUse large, confident headings.\n\nUse dramatic differences between:\n\n* Primary information\n* Secondary information\n* Supporting metadata\n\nUse whitespace intentionally.\n\nCreate visual rhythm through:\n\n* Typography\n* Spacing\n* Alignment\n* Scale\n* Horizontal rules\n* Image placement\n* Color blocks\n* Asymmetrical compositions\n\nDo not make every section perfectly symmetrical.\n\nThe design should feel editorial and art-directed while remaining extremely usable.\n\n---\n\n# 4. COLOR SYSTEM\n\nCreate a restrained color palette.\n\nUse:\n\n* Warm/off-white as the primary background\n* Near-black/dark charcoal for primary text\n* One strong signature accent color\n* Very limited secondary colors\n\nThe accent color should be used intentionally for:\n\n* Primary actions\n* Active navigation\n* Important states\n* Selected content\n* Key visual moments\n\nDo not use multiple competing accent colors.\n\nDo not use purple/blue as the default AI color scheme.\n\nThe interface should be predominantly neutral with strategic moments of bold color.\n\n---\n\n# 5. TYPOGRAPHY\n\nTypography is one of the most important parts of the design.\n\nUse a modern, premium sans-serif typeface.\n\nRecommended direction:\n\n* Inter\n* Geist\n* Satoshi\n* Manrope\n* A similar high-quality modern sans-serif\n\nUse:\n\n* Large display typography for major page headings\n* Medium/strong typography for section headings\n* Compact typography for metadata\n* Clear readable body text\n\nCreate a strong typographic hierarchy.\n\nDo not use oversized typography everywhere.\n\nLarge type should appear intentionally and create visual impact.\n\n---\n\n# 6. LAYOUT\n\nUse a spacious application layout.\n\nDesktop:\n\n* Slim, elegant navigation\n* Large content area\n* Generous margins\n* Strong alignment\n* Clear visual hierarchy\n\nAvoid a bulky traditional dashboard sidebar.\n\nThe navigation should feel lightweight and refined.\n\nUse horizontal rules and spacing to separate sections when appropriate instead of relying on cards.\n\n---\n\n# 7. APPLICATION STRUCTURE\n\nCreate the following primary sections:\n\n### Dashboard\n\nThe dashboard should immediately communicate:\n\n* What the creator has recently added\n* Content waiting to be worked on\n* Recently generated captions\n* Upcoming posts\n* Quick actions\n\nDo not make this a traditional analytics dashboard.\n\nThe dashboard should feel more like a **creative workspace**.\n\n---\n\n### Content Library\n\nCreate a visually rich content library.\n\nUsers should be able to:\n\n* View images\n* View videos\n* Search content\n* Filter content\n* Sort content\n* Select content\n* Preview content\n* Organize content\n\nUse large visual previews.\n\nImages and videos should be treated as important visual elements rather than tiny thumbnails inside cards.\n\n---\n\n### Google Drive\n\nCreate a clean connection flow for Google Drive.\n\nThe user should be able to:\n\n1. Connect Google Drive\n2. Authorize access\n3. Select a folder\n4. Scan the folder\n5. Import/discover content\n6. See synchronization status\n\nMake this flow extremely simple.\n\nDo not overwhelm the user with technical information.\n\n---\n\n### AI Caption Studio\n\nThis should be one of the most visually important screens.\n\nThe user selects a piece of content.\n\nThen the application presents:\n\nIMAGE / VIDEO\n\nalongside:\n\nGENERATED CAPTION\n\nThe caption should be editable.\n\nInclude controls for:\n\n* Platform\n* Tone\n* Caption length\n* Audience\n* Call to action\n* Hashtags\n\nAllow the user to regenerate the caption.\n\nThe interface should feel like a **creative writing workspace**, not a chatbot.\n\nDo NOT create a giant chat interface.\n\nThe AI should feel embedded into the product.\n\n---\n\n### Post Editor\n\nCreate a polished editing environment.\n\nThe creator should be able to:\n\n* Edit caption\n* Select platform\n* Add hashtags\n* Add CTA\n* Preview post\n* Save draft\n* Schedule/publish when integrations are available\n\nThe preview should resemble the destination social post without pretending to be an exact clone of the platform.\n\n---\n\n### Content Calendar\n\nCreate a clean visual calendar.\n\nUsers should be able to see:\n\n* Drafts\n* Scheduled content\n* Published content\n\nUse color sparingly to distinguish states.\n\nAvoid filling the calendar with excessive cards.\n\n---\n\n# 8. COMPONENT DESIGN\n\nCreate a reusable design system.\n\nComponents should include:\n\n* Buttons\n* Inputs\n* Dropdowns\n* Tabs\n* Navigation\n* Modal\n* Drawer\n* Toast\n* Search\n* Filters\n* Media cards\n* Content rows\n* Empty states\n* Loading states\n* Error states\n* Confirmation states\n\nMaintain consistent:\n\n* Border radius\n* Spacing\n* Typography\n* Button heights\n* Icon sizes\n* Borders\n* Interaction states\n\nDo not randomly change component styling between pages.\n\n---\n\n# 9. BUTTONS\n\nButtons should be simple and confident.\n\nPrimary buttons:\n\n* Strong contrast\n* Clear typography\n* Moderate radius\n* No gradients\n\nSecondary buttons:\n\n* Minimal\n* Border-based or text-based\n\nAvoid making every action a large colorful button.\n\nHierarchy matters.\n\n---\n\n# 10. CARDS\n\nCards should be used selectively.\n\nDo NOT put everything inside cards.\n\nUse cards when they genuinely improve grouping or interaction.\n\nFor content/media, prioritize:\n\n* Image\n* Typography\n* Spacing\n* Metadata\n\nrather than excessive borders and shadows.\n\n---\n\n# 11. MOTION\n\nAdd subtle premium motion.\n\nUse animation for:\n\n* Page transitions\n* Hover states\n* Content loading\n* Modal opening\n* Caption generation\n* Content importing\n* Navigation transitions\n* Drag interactions\n\nMotion should feel:\n\n* Smooth\n* Fast\n* Intentional\n* Premium\n\nDo NOT use excessive animation.\n\nNo distracting floating objects.\n\n---\n\n# 12. EMPTY STATES\n\nDesign beautiful empty states.\n\nExamples:\n\nNo content:\n\n\"Your creative library is empty.\"\n\nThen explain what the user can do next.\n\nNo captions:\n\n\"Nothing written yet.\"\n\nThen provide a clear action:\n\n\"Generate your first caption\"\n\nDo not use generic sad illustrations.\n\nUse typography and composition.\n\n---\n\n# 13. LOADING STATES\n\nLoading states should feel intentional.\n\nExamples:\n\nWhen scanning Google Drive:\n\n\"Looking through your library...\"\n\nWhen generating a caption:\n\n\"Finding the right words...\"\n\nUse subtle animation.\n\nDo not show generic spinning loaders everywhere.\n\n---\n\n# 14. RESPONSIVE DESIGN\n\nThe application must work beautifully on:\n\n* Desktop\n* Laptop\n* Tablet\n* Mobile\n\nDo not simply shrink the desktop interface.\n\nReorganize the layout appropriately for smaller screens.\n\nNavigation should transform intelligently.\n\nMedia should remain visually prominent.\n\nTypography should scale appropriately.\n\n---\n\n# 15. UX PRINCIPLES\n\nThe application should always answer:\n\n1. Where am I?\n2. What can I do here?\n3. What should I do next?\n4. What just happened?\n\nEvery screen should have one clear primary action.\n\nReduce unnecessary steps.\n\nDo not overload users with configuration.\n\nKeep the interface intuitive for creators who are not technical.\n\n---\n\n# 16. DASHBOARD EXPERIENCE\n\nThe dashboard should feel like opening a creative workspace.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"Total Posts: 34\"\n\n\"Engagement: 12,431\"\n\n\"Followers: 4,823\"\n\nwith ten generic analytics cards,\n\nprioritize:\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\n\"Here\'s what you\'ve been working on.\"\n\nThen show:\n\n* Recent content\n* Content waiting for captions\n* Upcoming posts\n* Recently generated captions\n* Quick actions\n\nMake the product feel personal and creative.\n\n---\n\n# 17. MICROCOPY\n\nUse short, confident, human language.\n\nAvoid robotic SaaS language.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"Click the button below to initiate the process.\"\n\nUse:\n\n\"Connect your Drive.\"\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"AI caption generation has been successfully completed.\"\n\nUse:\n\n\"Your caption is ready.\"\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"No records found.\"\n\nUse:\n\n\"Nothing here yet.\"\n\nThe interface should sound human.\n\n---\n\n# 18. DESIGN QUALITY STANDARD\n\nBefore considering any screen complete, evaluate:\n\n* Is the hierarchy obvious?\n* Does the screen feel visually distinctive?\n* Is there unnecessary UI?\n* Are there too many cards?\n* Are there too many colors?\n* Is typography doing enough work?\n* Does the page feel spacious?\n* Does the primary action stand out?\n* Does it look like a real premium product?\n* Does it look like something an experienced design studio created?\n\nIf the result looks like a typical AI-generated SaaS dashboard, redesign it.\n\n---\n\n# 19. IMPLEMENTATION RULE\n\nBuild the application using a reusable component architecture.\n\nDo not create isolated styles for every page.\n\nCreate a consistent design system and reuse it throughout the application.\n\nBefore implementing the complete application, establish:\n\n* Design tokens\n* Typography scale\n* Color tokens\n* Spacing system\n* Border system\n* Radius system\n* Button system\n* Form system\n* Navigation system\n* Card/media system\n\nThen use those systems consistently.\n\n---\n\n# 20. IMPORTANT \342\200\224 DO NOT CODE EVERYTHING AT ONCE\n\nFirst create the visual foundation.\n\nBuild these screens first:\n\n1. Dashboard\n2. Content Library\n3. AI Caption Studio\n4. Post Editor\n\nMake these four screens visually excellent.\n\nDo not proceed to advanced integrations until the visual system is consistent across these screens.\n\nUse realistic sample content so the interface looks like a functioning product rather than a wireframe.\n\nAfter the UI is approved, continue with:\n\nGoogle Drive integration \342\206\222 AI caption generation \342\206\222 authentication \342\206\222 database \342\206\222 scheduling/publishing \342\206\222 remaining functionality.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL DESIGN GOAL\n\nThe final application should feel like:\n\n**Kickpush-inspired creative direction + premium SaaS usability + modern creator tool + intelligent AI functionality.**\n\nIt should be visually bold without being noisy.\n\nMinimal without being empty.\n\nCreative without being confusing.\n\nProfessional without being corporate.\n\nAnd most importantly:\n\n**It must NOT look like an AI-generated interface.**"
    items {
      text: "You are a senior product designer, UI/UX designer, and frontend design engineer.\n\nI am building a modern SaaS application for content creators. The application helps creators connect their Google Drive, discover and organize their content, generate high-quality social media captions with AI, edit content, and prepare posts for publishing.\n\nUse the **Kickpush Design Studio website on Awwwards** as the primary visual inspiration:\n\nhttps://www.awwwards.com/sites/kickpush-design-studio\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo NOT copy the Kickpush website.\n\nDo NOT reproduce its exact layouts, text, branding, illustrations, images, or proprietary assets.\n\nInstead, extract and translate its **design principles, visual confidence, typography, spacing, composition, color usage, minimalism, and creative-agency aesthetic** into a completely original SaaS application.\n\nThe final product should feel like a premium digital product designed by an award-winning creative studio \342\200\224 NOT like a generic AI-generated dashboard.\n\n---\n\n# 1. CORE DESIGN DIRECTION\n\nThe visual identity should be:\n\n* Minimal\n* Bold\n* Editorial\n* Creative\n* Typography-driven\n* Confident\n* Modern\n* Premium\n* Slightly unconventional\n* Highly intentional\n* Clean without feeling boring\n* Playful without becoming childish\n* Professional without feeling corporate\n\nThe interface should communicate:\n\n> \"This is a beautifully designed creative tool.\"\n\nIt should NOT communicate:\n\n> \"This is another AI SaaS template.\"\n\n---\n\n# 2. ABSOLUTELY AVOID GENERIC AI UI\n\nDo NOT use:\n\n* Purple-to-blue gradients\n* Generic AI dashboard aesthetics\n* Excessive glassmorphism\n* Floating gradient blobs\n* Excessive shadows\n* Excessive rounded cards\n* Every section inside a card\n* Huge collections of identical cards\n* Generic SaaS templates\n* Excessive icons\n* Decorative UI that has no purpose\n* Random gradients\n* Neon effects\n* Excessive pills\n* Excessive badges\n* Overly rounded buttons\n* Stock-looking illustrations\n* AI-generated decorative graphics\n\nDo not make every component look like a floating white card.\n\nUse **space, typography, borders, lines, scale, color blocks, imagery and composition** to create hierarchy.\n\n---\n\n# 3. VISUAL PHILOSOPHY\n\nTypography should do a significant amount of the visual work.\n\nUse large, confident headings.\n\nUse dramatic differences between:\n\n* Primary information\n* Secondary information\n* Supporting metadata\n\nUse whitespace intentionally.\n\nCreate visual rhythm through:\n\n* Typography\n* Spacing\n* Alignment\n* Scale\n* Horizontal rules\n* Image placement\n* Color blocks\n* Asymmetrical compositions\n\nDo not make every section perfectly symmetrical.\n\nThe design should feel editorial and art-directed while remaining extremely usable.\n\n---\n\n# 4. COLOR SYSTEM\n\nCreate a restrained color palette.\n\nUse:\n\n* Warm/off-white as the primary background\n* Near-black/dark charcoal for primary text\n* One strong signature accent color\n* Very limited secondary colors\n\nThe accent color should be used intentionally for:\n\n* Primary actions\n* Active navigation\n* Important states\n* Selected content\n* Key visual moments\n\nDo not use multiple competing accent colors.\n\nDo not use purple/blue as the default AI color scheme.\n\nThe interface should be predominantly neutral with strategic moments of bold color.\n\n---\n\n# 5. TYPOGRAPHY\n\nTypography is one of the most important parts of the design.\n\nUse a modern, premium sans-serif typeface.\n\nRecommended direction:\n\n* Inter\n* Geist\n* Satoshi\n* Manrope\n* A similar high-quality modern sans-serif\n\nUse:\n\n* Large display typography for major page headings\n* Medium/strong typography for section headings\n* Compact typography for metadata\n* Clear readable body text\n\nCreate a strong typographic hierarchy.\n\nDo not use oversized typography everywhere.\n\nLarge type should appear intentionally and create visual impact.\n\n---\n\n# 6. LAYOUT\n\nUse a spacious application layout.\n\nDesktop:\n\n* Slim, elegant navigation\n* Large content area\n* Generous margins\n* Strong alignment\n* Clear visual hierarchy\n\nAvoid a bulky traditional dashboard sidebar.\n\nThe navigation should feel lightweight and refined.\n\nUse horizontal rules and spacing to separate sections when appropriate instead of relying on cards.\n\n---\n\n# 7. APPLICATION STRUCTURE\n\nCreate the following primary sections:\n\n### Dashboard\n\nThe dashboard should immediately communicate:\n\n* What the creator has recently added\n* Content waiting to be worked on\n* Recently generated captions\n* Upcoming posts\n* Quick actions\n\nDo not make this a traditional analytics dashboard.\n\nThe dashboard should feel more like a **creative workspace**.\n\n---\n\n### Content Library\n\nCreate a visually rich content library.\n\nUsers should be able to:\n\n* View images\n* View videos\n* Search content\n* Filter content\n* Sort content\n* Select content\n* Preview content\n* Organize content\n\nUse large visual previews.\n\nImages and videos should be treated as important visual elements rather than tiny thumbnails inside cards.\n\n---\n\n### Google Drive\n\nCreate a clean connection flow for Google Drive.\n\nThe user should be able to:\n\n1. Connect Google Drive\n2. Authorize access\n3. Select a folder\n4. Scan the folder\n5. Import/discover content\n6. See synchronization status\n\nMake this flow extremely simple.\n\nDo not overwhelm the user with technical information.\n\n---\n\n### AI Caption Studio\n\nThis should be one of the most visually important screens.\n\nThe user selects a piece of content.\n\nThen the application presents:\n\nIMAGE / VIDEO\n\nalongside:\n\nGENERATED CAPTION\n\nThe caption should be editable.\n\nInclude controls for:\n\n* Platform\n* Tone\n* Caption length\n* Audience\n* Call to action\n* Hashtags\n\nAllow the user to regenerate the caption.\n\nThe interface should feel like a **creative writing workspace**, not a chatbot.\n\nDo NOT create a giant chat interface.\n\nThe AI should feel embedded into the product.\n\n---\n\n### Post Editor\n\nCreate a polished editing environment.\n\nThe creator should be able to:\n\n* Edit caption\n* Select platform\n* Add hashtags\n* Add CTA\n* Preview post\n* Save draft\n* Schedule/publish when integrations are available\n\nThe preview should resemble the destination social post without pretending to be an exact clone of the platform.\n\n---\n\n### Content Calendar\n\nCreate a clean visual calendar.\n\nUsers should be able to see:\n\n* Drafts\n* Scheduled content\n* Published content\n\nUse color sparingly to distinguish states.\n\nAvoid filling the calendar with excessive cards.\n\n---\n\n# 8. COMPONENT DESIGN\n\nCreate a reusable design system.\n\nComponents should include:\n\n* Buttons\n* Inputs\n* Dropdowns\n* Tabs\n* Navigation\n* Modal\n* Drawer\n* Toast\n* Search\n* Filters\n* Media cards\n* Content rows\n* Empty states\n* Loading states\n* Error states\n* Confirmation states\n\nMaintain consistent:\n\n* Border radius\n* Spacing\n* Typography\n* Button heights\n* Icon sizes\n* Borders\n* Interaction states\n\nDo not randomly change component styling between pages.\n\n---\n\n# 9. BUTTONS\n\nButtons should be simple and confident.\n\nPrimary buttons:\n\n* Strong contrast\n* Clear typography\n* Moderate radius\n* No gradients\n\nSecondary buttons:\n\n* Minimal\n* Border-based or text-based\n\nAvoid making every action a large colorful button.\n\nHierarchy matters.\n\n---\n\n# 10. CARDS\n\nCards should be used selectively.\n\nDo NOT put everything inside cards.\n\nUse cards when they genuinely improve grouping or interaction.\n\nFor content/media, prioritize:\n\n* Image\n* Typography\n* Spacing\n* Metadata\n\nrather than excessive borders and shadows.\n\n---\n\n# 11. MOTION\n\nAdd subtle premium motion.\n\nUse animation for:\n\n* Page transitions\n* Hover states\n* Content loading\n* Modal opening\n* Caption generation\n* Content importing\n* Navigation transitions\n* Drag interactions\n\nMotion should feel:\n\n* Smooth\n* Fast\n* Intentional\n* Premium\n\nDo NOT use excessive animation.\n\nNo distracting floating objects.\n\n---\n\n# 12. EMPTY STATES\n\nDesign beautiful empty states.\n\nExamples:\n\nNo content:\n\n\"Your creative library is empty.\"\n\nThen explain what the user can do next.\n\nNo captions:\n\n\"Nothing written yet.\"\n\nThen provide a clear action:\n\n\"Generate your first caption\"\n\nDo not use generic sad illustrations.\n\nUse typography and composition.\n\n---\n\n# 13. LOADING STATES\n\nLoading states should feel intentional.\n\nExamples:\n\nWhen scanning Google Drive:\n\n\"Looking through your library...\"\n\nWhen generating a caption:\n\n\"Finding the right words...\"\n\nUse subtle animation.\n\nDo not show generic spinning loaders everywhere.\n\n---\n\n# 14. RESPONSIVE DESIGN\n\nThe application must work beautifully on:\n\n* Desktop\n* Laptop\n* Tablet\n* Mobile\n\nDo not simply shrink the desktop interface.\n\nReorganize the layout appropriately for smaller screens.\n\nNavigation should transform intelligently.\n\nMedia should remain visually prominent.\n\nTypography should scale appropriately.\n\n---\n\n# 15. UX PRINCIPLES\n\nThe application should always answer:\n\n1. Where am I?\n2. What can I do here?\n3. What should I do next?\n4. What just happened?\n\nEvery screen should have one clear primary action.\n\nReduce unnecessary steps.\n\nDo not overload users with configuration.\n\nKeep the interface intuitive for creators who are not technical.\n\n---\n\n# 16. DASHBOARD EXPERIENCE\n\nThe dashboard should feel like opening a creative workspace.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"Total Posts: 34\"\n\n\"Engagement: 12,431\"\n\n\"Followers: 4,823\"\n\nwith ten generic analytics cards,\n\nprioritize:\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\n\"Here\'s what you\'ve been working on.\"\n\nThen show:\n\n* Recent content\n* Content waiting for captions\n* Upcoming posts\n* Recently generated captions\n* Quick actions\n\nMake the product feel personal and creative.\n\n---\n\n# 17. MICROCOPY\n\nUse short, confident, human language.\n\nAvoid robotic SaaS language.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"Click the button below to initiate the process.\"\n\nUse:\n\n\"Connect your Drive.\"\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"AI caption generation has been successfully completed.\"\n\nUse:\n\n\"Your caption is ready.\"\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"No records found.\"\n\nUse:\n\n\"Nothing here yet.\"\n\nThe interface should sound human.\n\n---\n\n# 18. DESIGN QUALITY STANDARD\n\nBefore considering any screen complete, evaluate:\n\n* Is the hierarchy obvious?\n* Does the screen feel visually distinctive?\n* Is there unnecessary UI?\n* Are there too many cards?\n* Are there too many colors?\n* Is typography doing enough work?\n* Does the page feel spacious?\n* Does the primary action stand out?\n* Does it look like a real premium product?\n* Does it look like something an experienced design studio created?\n\nIf the result looks like a typical AI-generated SaaS dashboard, redesign it.\n\n---\n\n# 19. IMPLEMENTATION RULE\n\nBuild the application using a reusable component architecture.\n\nDo not create isolated styles for every page.\n\nCreate a consistent design system and reuse it throughout the application.\n\nBefore implementing the complete application, establish:\n\n* Design tokens\n* Typography scale\n* Color tokens\n* Spacing system\n* Border system\n* Radius system\n* Button system\n* Form system\n* Navigation system\n* Card/media system\n\nThen use those systems consistently.\n\n---\n\n# 20. IMPORTANT \342\200\224 DO NOT CODE EVERYTHING AT ONCE\n\nFirst create the visual foundation.\n\nBuild these screens first:\n\n1. Dashboard\n2. Content Library\n3. AI Caption Studio\n4. Post Editor\n\nMake these four screens visually excellent.\n\nDo not proceed to advanced integrations until the visual system is consistent across these screens.\n\nUse realistic sample content so the interface looks like a functioning product rather than a wireframe.\n\nAfter the UI is approved, continue with:\n\nGoogle Drive integration \342\206\222 AI caption generation \342\206\222 authentication \342\206\222 database \342\206\222 scheduling/publishing \342\206\222 remaining functionality.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL DESIGN GOAL\n\nThe final application should feel like:\n\n**Kickpush-inspired creative direction + premium SaaS usability + modern creator tool + intelligent AI functionality.**\n\nIt should be visually bold without being noisy.\n\nMinimal without being empty.\n\nCreative without being confusing.\n\nProfessional without being corporate.\n\nAnd most importantly:\n\n**It must NOT look like an AI-generated interface.**"
